// Stage 2c: cache-loss sensitivity across a warm reboot.
//
// On a host whose PMEM is DRAM reserved with memmap=<size>!<start>, the
// reserved range survives a warm reboot (power is never removed) while a CPU
// reset discards cache contents without writing them back. A line that was
// stored but never written back is therefore lost across
// `echo b > /proc/sysrq-trigger`, and a line that was CLWB-ed and fenced
// survives. That is the discrimination the Magpie crash loop lacks: killing a
// process never discards the cache. See docs/pmem-emulation.md, Stage 2c, and
// docs/stage2c-handoff.md for the runbook.
//
// This program does the work on either side of a reboot; the reboot itself is
// triggered by the operator (scripts/stage2c.sh prints the command). Every
// mode except the verifiers writes a small state file and fsyncs it after each
// line, because a sysrq reboot does not sync disks. It never syncs the DAX
// filesystem: on a DAX file that could write back exactly the lines under test.
//
//   probe-arm <dax-dir> <state-file> <file|pmem> [bytes] [reset-fd]
//       Direct sensitivity probe, independent of the WAL. Writes a fresh file:
//       even cache lines are stored, CLWB-ed and fenced; odd lines are only
//       stored. Both carry a per-run nonce, so stale content cannot pass.
//       With reset-fd (an inherited descriptor for /proc/sysrq-trigger), the
//       probe writes 'b' to it straight after its last store, so no window
//       is left in which the unflushed lines could be evicted.
//   probe-verify <probe-file> <bytes> <nonce>
//       After the reboot: counts intact, torn and lost lines in each set.
//
//   setup <dax-dir> <state-file> <file|pmem|direct> [blocks] [blockSize] [steps]
//       Formats a fresh region and runs the sieve for a few steps with the
//       production provider, then closes cleanly, so this state is durable
//       before the experiment starts. It doubles as the flushed control.
//   arm <region> <state-file> <file|pmem|direct> <ordinary|elide-clwb> <blocks> <blockSize> [steps] [reset-fd]
//       Remounts with the chosen provider and runs more steps, recording each
//       acknowledged step, then exits WITHOUT closing: no clean-close
//       writeback, exactly as a crash would leave it. With reset-fd, as for
//       probe-arm, it resets the machine instead of exiting, straight after
//       the fsync that makes the last acknowledgement durable.
//   verify <region> <file|pmem|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <setup-cursor> <acked-cursor> <acked-count>
//       After the reboot: checks the raw image before mounting (RegionAllocator
//       formats a region whose header is missing, which would destroy the
//       evidence), then mounts, recovers, and checks acknowledged progress,
//       the allocator and sieve invariants, and the prime count.
//
// The same three sieve modes also accept `direct`, the pwrite + O_DIRECT
// backend over a private staging buffer. There no reboot is needed: process
// exit already discards whatever was never written back, so setup -> arm ->
// verify is the whole experiment, with the crash straight after the last
// acknowledgement. The probe modes drive clwb/sfence on the provider directly
// and have no meaning for it, so they refuse it.
//
// Three more modes drive a failed persistence boundary under a real EIO
// from the block layer, for scripts/desktop-fsyncfail-check.sh, which maps
// the log block to device sectors and fails writes there while fault-commit
// waits. File and direct backends only: the question is the page cache.
//
//   fault-prepare <dir> <state-file> <file|direct> [blocks] [blockSize] [steps] [scenario]
//       As setup; for the file backend it then rewrites the log block's own
//       bytes and fdatasyncs, so every page of it is allocated and written
//       before a fault is armed (the direct backend's format writes every
//       unit). Records the log block's offset and length and the marker word.
//       Scenario single (the default) stops at the clean close. stale-slot and
//       tear then mount the closed region again (CLEAN, so the next sequence
//       is 1), commit N-2 (the marker word M = A) and N-1 (M = B), call
//       flush(), which persists the applied after-images and scrubs nothing,
//       and exit WITHOUT closing: both slots keep a valid record and the data
//       is durable. A read-only WAL instance then checks both slots.
//   fault-commit <region> <file|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <signal-dir> [timeout-s [scenario]]
//       One process: mounts, prints READY, waits for <signal-dir>/go-commit,
//       commits one transaction storing a fresh nonce in the marker word,
//       prints the result and the latch, prints ATTEMPTED, waits for
//       <signal-dir>/go-reopen, then abandons that mount unclosed and opens a
//       new instance (the production abandon, reopen and recover), prints its
//       recovery and whether the nonce is visible, and exits without closing.
//       stale-slot and tear: the mount replays N-2 and N-1; transaction N
//       stores M = C (tear: and the second word Z = E); before each mount the
//       slots are read through a read-only WAL instance and printed, with the
//       slot N mod 2 under test and both slots' offsets. stale-slot then
//       prints REOPENED, waits for <signal-dir>/go-next, and commits "next",
//       which stores the second word M2 = D, and prints its result and
//       sequence.
//   fault-verify <region> <file|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <nonce> <setup-cursor> <setup-count>
//       Later, in a new process: mounts, recovers, reports the marker, and
//       runs the sieve and allocator checks.
//   fault-verify-acks <region> <file|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <stale-slot|tear>
//                     <setup-cursor> <setup-count> <a> <b> <c> <x> <next-seq>
//       The same for the two-record scenarios: prints the slots' sequences as
//       found, mounts, reports M and the second word, runs the checks, and
//       judges the acknowledgements (judgeAcks() below). <x> is D for
//       stale-slot and E for tear; <next-seq> is next's sequence, 0 if next
//       was not acknowledged (always 0 for tear).
//   fault-verdict-test
//       Feeds judgeAcks() the predicted and the other possible outcomes of
//       both scenarios and checks every verdict.
//
// The marker word is the base field of the sieve root chunk's last descriptor
// slot, which no step of these runs reaches (2 of 167 slots at 4 KiB blocks,
// 2 of 337 at 8 KiB); checkInvariants() reads only the first numSegments
// descriptors, so the transaction under test changes nothing the workload
// checks. The second word is the primeCount field of the same descriptor, for
// the same reason.
//
// Exit status: 0 success (verify: SURVIVED; probe-verify: SENSITIVE), 1 LOST
// (acknowledged state did not survive), 2 usage, 3 INVALID (the flushed
// control state itself is gone, so the run says nothing about flush
// placement), 4 NOT-SENSITIVE (probe-verify: every unflushed line survived).
// fault-commit exits 0 once its protocol completes, whatever the outcome (the
// outcome is in its output), and 1 if the protocol could not be completed;
// fault-verify exits 0 CONSISTENT, 1 INCONSISTENT, 3 INVALID;
// fault-verify-acks exits 0 CONSISTENT, 1 LOST, 3 INVALID, 5 TORN,
// 6 INCONSISTENT; fault-verdict-test exits 0 if every verdict matched.

def LIVE_SEGMENTS = 2u64;
def MAX_REFERENCE = 64000000u64;
def LINE = 64;

def EXIT_OK = 0;
def EXIT_LOST = 1;
def EXIT_USAGE = 2;
def EXIT_INVALID = 3;
def EXIT_NOT_SENSITIVE = 4;
def EXIT_TORN = 5;
def EXIT_INCONSISTENT = 6;

component RebootConfig {
        def var usePmem = false;
        def var useDirect = false;
        def var elideWriteback = false;
        def var path: string;
}

def usage() -> int {
        System.puts("Usage:\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot probe-arm <dax-dir> <state-file> <file|pmem> [bytes] [reset-fd]\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot probe-verify <probe-file> <bytes> <nonce>\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot setup <dax-dir> <state-file> <file|pmem|direct> [blocks] [blockSize] [steps]\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot arm <region> <state-file> <file|pmem|direct> <ordinary|elide-clwb> <blocks> <blockSize> [steps] [reset-fd]\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot verify <region> <file|pmem|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <setup-cursor> <acked-cursor> <acked-count>\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot fault-prepare <dir> <state-file> <file|direct> [blocks] [blockSize] [steps] [single|stale-slot|tear]\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot fault-commit <region> <file|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <signal-dir> [timeout-s [single|stale-slot|tear]]\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot fault-verify <region> <file|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <nonce> <setup-cursor> <setup-count>\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot fault-verify-acks <region> <file|direct> <blocks> <blockSize> <stale-slot|tear> <setup-cursor> <setup-count> <a> <b> <c> <x> <next-seq>\n");
        System.puts("  pwreboot fault-verdict-test\n");
        return EXIT_USAGE;
}

def parseU64(s: string, dflt: u64) -> u64 {
        if (s == null || s.length == 0) return dflt;
        var v: u64 = 0;
        for (i < s.length) {
                def c = s[i];
                if (c < '0' || c > '9') return dflt;
                v = v * 10u64 + u64.!(c - '0');
        }
        return v;
}

// Strict: a malformed number in a verify argument must not become a default
// that happens to pass.
def requireU64(s: string, what: string) -> (bool, u64) {
        if (s == null || s.length == 0) return (false, 0);
        for (i < s.length) {
                if (s[i] < '0' || s[i] > '9') {
                        System.puts("not a number for ");
                        System.puts(what);
                        System.puts(": ");
                        System.puts(s);
                        System.ln();
                        return (false, 0);
                }
        }
        return (true, parseU64(s, 0));
}

def parseBackend(s: string) -> bool {
        RebootConfig.usePmem = Strings.equal(s, "pmem");
        RebootConfig.useDirect = Strings.equal(s, "direct");
        if (RebootConfig.usePmem || RebootConfig.useDirect || Strings.equal(s, "file")) return true;
        System.puts("backend must be \"file\", \"pmem\" or \"direct\"\n");
        return false;
}

def backendLabel() -> string {
        if (RebootConfig.usePmem) return "pmem";
        if (RebootConfig.useDirect) return "direct";
        return "file";
}

def parseProvider(s: string) -> bool {
        if (Strings.equal(s, "elide-clwb")) { RebootConfig.elideWriteback = true; return true; }
        if (Strings.equal(s, "ordinary")) { RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false; return true; }
        System.puts("writeback mode must be \"ordinary\" or \"elide-clwb\"\n");
        return false;
}

def openBackend() -> StorageBackend {
        var backend: StorageBackend;
        if (RebootConfig.usePmem) backend = X86_64Backends.pmem(RebootConfig.path);
        else if (RebootConfig.useDirect) backend = DirectIoBackend.new(RebootConfig.path, DirectIoTarget.FILE);
        else backend = X86_64Backends.mappedFile(RebootConfig.path);
        if (RebootConfig.elideWriteback) return ElidedWriteBackBackend.new(backend);
        return backend;
}

// The same anti-fallback checks as the crash loop: a file mapping reported as
// a PMEM run, or an ordinary provider reported as the mutant, would invert the
// comparison this experiment exists to make.
def checkStorageRegion(region: StorageRegion) -> string {
        if (region == null) return "backend region could not be created";
        if (RebootConfig.usePmem && !PmemRegion.?(region)) return "pmem mode did not produce a PmemRegion";
        if (RebootConfig.useDirect && !DirectIoRegion.?(region)) return "direct mode did not produce a DirectIoRegion";
        if (!RebootConfig.usePmem && !RebootConfig.useDirect && !MappedFileRegion.?(region)) return "file mode did not produce a MappedFileRegion";
        def elided = ElidedWriteBackProvider.?(region.provider());
        if (RebootConfig.elideWriteback && !elided) return "elide-clwb did not install the elided-writeback provider";
        if (!RebootConfig.elideWriteback && elided) return "an ordinary run installed the elided-writeback provider";
        return null;
}

def fail(msg: string) -> int {
        System.puts("FAIL: ");
        System.puts(msg);
        System.ln();
        return EXIT_LOST;
}

// Reserve a private, uniquely named file in the assigned directory, as the
// other runners do: O_EXCL, so an earlier run's evidence is never replaced.
def reservePath(dir: string, stem: string) -> string {
        def uid = Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_getuid, ()).0;
        def pid = Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_getpid, ()).0;
        for (attempt < 100) {
                def path = StringBuilder.new()
                        .puts(dir).puts("/").puts(stem).puts("-u").putd(uid)
                        .puts("-p").putd(pid).puts("-").putd(attempt).puts(".region")
                        .toString();
                def fd = BackingFile.createExclusive(path);
                if (fd >= 0) {
                        BackingFile.close(fd);
                        return path;
                }
                if (fd != -LinuxConst.EEXIST) return null;
        }
        return null;
}

// A state file on ordinary storage. Every line is fsynced as it is written:
// the operator reboots with sysrq, which does not sync, and an acknowledgement
// that did not reach the disk would make the verifier's reference point wrong.
class StateFile(fd: int) {
        def line(s: string) -> bool {
                def bytes = StringBuilder.new().puts(s).ln().toString();
                System.fileWriteK(fd, bytes, 0, bytes.length);
                return Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_fsync, fd).0 == 0;
        }
        def close() { BackingFile.close(fd); }
}

def openState(path: string) -> StateFile {
        def fd = BackingFile.create(path);
        if (fd < 0) return null;
        return StateFile.new(fd);
}

def kv(key: string, value: u64) -> string {
        return StringBuilder.new().puts(key).puts(" ").putd(value).toString();
}

def kvs(key: string, value: string) -> string {
        return StringBuilder.new().puts(key).puts(" ").puts(value).toString();
}

// The optional reset descriptor: an inherited descriptor for
// /proc/sysrq-trigger, or -1 when the operator resets the machine.
def parseResetFd(args: Array<string>, index: int) -> (bool, int) {
        if (args.length <= index) return (true, -1);
        def r = requireU64(args[index], "reset-fd");
        if (!r.0 || r.1 < 3u64 || r.1 > 1023u64) {
                System.puts("reset-fd must be an inherited descriptor, 3 or above\n");
                return (false, -1);
        }
        return (true, int.!(r.1));
}

// Reset the machine now, through the inherited descriptor. On
// /proc/sysrq-trigger the write does not return. If it does, the descriptor
// was a plain file, i.e. a plumbing dry run, and the caller carries on.
def requestReset(fd: int) -> bool {
        def key = "b";
        def r = Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_write, (fd, Pointer.atContents(key), 1)).0;
        if (r != 1) return false;
        System.puts("reset requested, but the machine is still running\n");
        return true;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ probe

// splitmix64 finaliser: a line's expected contents are a function of the run
// nonce and the line's position, so no earlier run's bytes can match.
def mix(x: u64) -> u64 {
        var z = x + 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15u64;
        z = (z ^ (z >> 30)) * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9u64;
        z = (z ^ (z >> 27)) * 0x94D049BB133111EBu64;
        return z ^ (z >> 31);
}

def expectedWord(nonce: u64, line: u64, w: u64) -> u64 {
        return mix(nonce ^ (line * 8u64 + w));
}

def randomNonce() -> u64 {
        def buf = Array<byte>.new(8);
        def r = Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_getrandom, (Pointer.atElement(buf, 0), 8, 0)).0;
        if (r != 8) return 0;
        var v: u64 = 0;
        for (i < 8) v = (v << 8) | u64.!(buf[i]);
        return v;
}

def probeArm(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 4) return usage();
        if (!parseBackend(args[3])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        if (RebootConfig.useDirect) {
                System.puts("the probe drives clwb/sfence itself; it has no meaning for the direct backend\n");
                return EXIT_USAGE;
        }
        def bytes = if(args.length > 4, parseU64(args[4], 0), 2u64 * 1024u64 * 1024u64);
        if (bytes < u64.!(2 * LINE) || bytes % 4096u64 != 0) {
                System.puts("probe size must be a positive multiple of 4096\n");
                return EXIT_USAGE;
        }
        def rf = parseResetFd(args, 5);
        if (!rf.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def resetFd = rf.1;
        def nonce = randomNonce();
        if (nonce == 0) return fail("getrandom failed");
        RebootConfig.path = reservePath(args[1], "wizard-pwreboot-probe");
        if (RebootConfig.path == null) return fail("could not reserve a probe file in the directory");
        def state = openState(args[2]);
        if (state == null) return fail("could not create the state file");

        def region = openBackend().open(bytes, BackendProt.READ.mask | BackendProt.WRITE.mask, OpenMode.FORMAT);
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        def ops = NativeProviders.installIfAbsent(region);
        def lines = bytes / u64.!(LINE);

        // Recorded before any store, so a reboot at any later point still
        // leaves the verifier everything it needs.
        state.line(kvs("probe_file", RebootConfig.path));
        state.line(kvs("backend", backendLabel()));
        state.line(kv("bytes", bytes));
        state.line(kv("nonce", nonce));
        state.line(kv("flushed_lines", lines / 2u64));
        state.line(kvs("reset", if(resetFd >= 0, "in-probe", "operator")));
        if (!state.line(kv("unflushed_lines", lines / 2u64))) return fail("could not sync the state file");

        // Flushed set first: every even line stored, written back and fenced.
        for (line = 0u64; line < lines; line += 2u64) {
                def base = line * u64.!(LINE);
                for (w = 0u64; w < 8u64; w++) ops.storeU64(base + w * 8u64, expectedWord(nonce, line, w));
                ops.writeBackLine(base);
        }
        ops.fence();
        // Unflushed set last, so the flushed set's traffic cannot evict it:
        // every odd line stored, never written back. The fence orders the
        // stores but does not make them durable.
        for (line = 1u64; line < lines; line += 2u64) {
                def base = line * u64.!(LINE);
                for (w = 0u64; w < 8u64; w++) ops.storeU64(base + w * 8u64, expectedWord(nonce, line, w));
        }
        ops.fence();
        // No window: nothing between the last store and the reset, not even
        // the state file's fsync, which could idle the CPU.
        if (resetFd >= 0 && !requestReset(resetFd)) return fail("could not write to the reset descriptor");
        state.line("armed yes");
        state.close();
        // Exit without unmapping explicitly: nothing here may write back the
        // unflushed set.
        System.puts("READY\n");
        return EXIT_OK;
}

def le64(a: Array<byte>, off: int) -> u64 {
        var v: u64 = 0;
        for (i = 7; i >= 0; i--) v = (v << 8) | u64.!(a[off + i]);
        return v;
}

def probeVerify(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 4) return usage();
        def b = requireU64(args[2], "bytes");
        def n = requireU64(args[3], "nonce");
        if (!b.0 || !n.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def bytes = b.1, nonce = n.1;
        def image = System.fileLoad(args[1]);
        if (image == null) {
                System.puts("verdict INVALID\nreason probe file is missing\n");
                return EXIT_INVALID;
        }
        if (u64.!(image.length) != bytes) {
                System.puts("verdict INVALID\nreason probe file has the wrong size\n");
                return EXIT_INVALID;
        }
        def lines = bytes / u64.!(LINE);
        // [set][intact, torn, lost, lost-and-zero]
        def counts = [Array<u64>.new(4), Array<u64>.new(4)];
        for (line = 0u64; line < lines; line++) {
                def set = int.!(line % 2u64);   // 0 = flushed, 1 = unflushed
                var matched = 0, zero = true;
                for (w = 0u64; w < 8u64; w++) {
                        def v = le64(image, int.!(line * u64.!(LINE) + w * 8u64));
                        if (v == expectedWord(nonce, line, w)) matched++;
                        if (v != 0) zero = false;
                }
                if (matched == 8) counts[set][0]++;
                else if (matched > 0) counts[set][1]++;
                else {
                        counts[set][2]++;
                        if (zero) counts[set][3]++;
                }
        }
        def names = ["flushed", "unflushed"];
        for (set < 2) {
                Trace.OUT.put3("%s lines: intact %d, torn %d", names[set], counts[set][0], counts[set][1]);
                Trace.OUT.put2(", lost %d (%d of them zero)", counts[set][2], counts[set][3]).ln();
        }
        // The flushed set is the control. If it did not survive intact, the
        // reserved memory itself did not survive the reboot, and the unflushed
        // set's fate says nothing about the cache.
        if (counts[0][0] != lines / 2u64) {
                System.puts("verdict INVALID\nreason flushed control lines were lost: the reserved memory did not survive the reboot intact\n");
                return EXIT_INVALID;
        }
        if (counts[1][0] == lines / 2u64) {
                System.puts("verdict NOT-SENSITIVE\nreason every unflushed line survived: the reset wrote the cache back, or it was evicted first\n");
                return EXIT_NOT_SENSITIVE;
        }
        System.puts("verdict SENSITIVE\nreason flushed lines all survived and unflushed lines were lost\n");
        return EXIT_OK;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ sieve

def openRegion(blocks: u64, blockSize: u64, fresh: bool) -> RegionAllocator {
        return RegionAllocator.new(openBackend(), blocks, Array<Ref<MetadataDescriptor>>.new(0), blockSize, if(fresh, OpenMode.FORMAT, OpenMode.ATTACH));
}

def setup(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 4) return usage();
        if (!parseBackend(args[3])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def blocks = if(args.length > 4, parseU64(args[4], 512), 512u64);
        def blockSize = if(args.length > 5, parseU64(args[5], 4096), 4096u64);
        def steps = if(args.length > 6, parseU64(args[6], 2), 2u64);
        RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false;    // the control state is always flushed
        RebootConfig.path = reservePath(args[1], "wizard-pwreboot");
        if (RebootConfig.path == null) return fail("could not reserve a region file in the directory");
        def state = openState(args[2]);
        if (state == null) return fail("could not create the state file");

        def region = openRegion(blocks, blockSize, true);
        if (region == null) return fail("could not create the region");
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(region, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return fail("could not lay out the sieve root");
        for (i < steps) {
                if (!sieve.step()) return fail("setup step failed");
        }
        def cursor = sieve.cursor(), count = sieve.count();
        // Clean close with the production provider: everything so far is
        // written back and fenced before the experiment begins.
        region.deallocate();

        state.line(kvs("region", RebootConfig.path));
        state.line(kvs("backend", backendLabel()));
        state.line(kv("blocks", blocks));
        state.line(kv("block_size", blockSize));
        state.line(kv("setup_steps", steps));
        state.line(kv("setup_cursor", cursor));
        if (!state.line(kv("setup_count", count))) return fail("could not sync the state file");
        state.close();
        Trace.OUT.put3("setup: %d steps, cursor %d, primes %d", steps, cursor, count).ln();
        System.puts("OK\n");
        return EXIT_OK;
}

def arm(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 7) return usage();
        RebootConfig.path = args[1];
        if (!parseBackend(args[3])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        if (!parseProvider(args[4])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def bl = requireU64(args[5], "blocks"), bs = requireU64(args[6], "blockSize");
        if (!bl.0 || !bs.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def steps = if(args.length > 7, parseU64(args[7], 3), 3u64);
        def rf = parseResetFd(args, 8);
        if (!rf.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def resetFd = rf.1;
        def state = openState(args[2]);
        if (state == null) return fail("could not create the state file");
        if (System.fileLoad(RebootConfig.path) == null) return fail("region file is missing; run setup first");

        def region = openRegion(bl.1, bs.1, false);
        if (region == null) return fail("could not mount the region");
        // A refused mount (another format version, or other geometry) leaves
        // no transaction; fail here rather than step an unmounted region.
        if (region.txn == null) return fail("the region did not mount: run setup again with this build and geometry");
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        if (region.requiresRecovery()) return fail("mount latched recovery-required");
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(region, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return fail("could not reattach to the sieve root");

        state.line(kvs("provider", if(RebootConfig.elideWriteback, "elide-clwb", "ordinary")));
        state.line(kv("start_cursor", sieve.cursor()));
        var done = 0u64;
        var lastAck: string;
        for (i < steps) {
                if (!sieve.step()) break;
                done++;
                // A step has returned, so its commits are acknowledged: the
                // contract says recovery must reach at least this far.
                def ack = StringBuilder.new().puts("ack ").putd(done)
                        .puts(" cursor ").putd(sieve.cursor())
                        .puts(" count ").putd(sieve.count()).toString();
                // The last acknowledgement goes out with the summary, in one
                // write and one fsync: with an in-process reset, that fsync is
                // all that separates the last step from the reset.
                if (done < steps) state.line(ack);
                else lastAck = ack;
        }
        def summary = StringBuilder.new();
        if (lastAck != null) summary.puts(lastAck).ln();
        summary.puts("acked_steps ").putd(done).ln()
                .puts("acked_cursor ").putd(sieve.cursor()).ln()
                .puts("acked_count ").putd(sieve.count());
        if (!state.line(summary.toString())) return fail("could not sync the state file");
        if (done == 0) return fail("no step completed, so nothing is under test");
        // No deallocate(): a clean close would write back and fence the very
        // state the mutant leaves in cache. Leave it exactly as a crash would.
        if (resetFd >= 0 && !requestReset(resetFd)) return fail("could not write to the reset descriptor");
        state.close();
        Trace.OUT.put3("armed: %d acknowledged steps, cursor %d, primes %d",
                done, sieve.cursor(), sieve.count()).ln();
        System.puts("READY\n");
        return EXIT_OK;
}

// An independent sieve of [0, limit), unrelated to PWSieve's implementation.
def referenceCount(limit: u64) -> u64 {
        if (limit < 2u64) return 0;
        def n = int.!(limit);
        def composite = Array<bool>.new(n);
        var count: u64 = 0;
        for (p = 2; p < n; p++) {
                if (composite[p]) continue;
                count++;
                if (p > n / p) continue;
                var m = p * p;
                while (m < n) {
                        composite[m] = true;
                        m += p;
                }
        }
        return count;
}

def verdict(kind: string, reason: string, code: int) -> int {
        System.puts("verdict ");
        System.puts(kind);
        System.puts("\nreason ");
        System.puts(reason);
        System.ln();
        return code;
}

def verify(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 8) return usage();
        RebootConfig.path = args[1];
        if (!parseBackend(args[2])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false;    // recovery is always the production code
        def bl = requireU64(args[3], "blocks"), bs = requireU64(args[4], "blockSize");
        def sc = requireU64(args[5], "setup-cursor"), ac = requireU64(args[6], "acked-cursor");
        def an = requireU64(args[7], "acked-count");
        if (!bl.0 || !bs.0 || !sc.0 || !ac.0 || !an.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def blocks = bl.1, blockSize = bs.1, setupCursor = sc.1, ackedCursor = ac.1, ackedCount = an.1;

        // Inspect the raw image first. RegionAllocator formats a region whose header
        // magic is missing, so mounting before this check would erase the
        // evidence and report a fresh, empty region.
        def image = System.fileLoad(RebootConfig.path);
        if (image == null) return verdict("INVALID", "region file is missing", EXIT_INVALID);
        if (u64.!(image.length) != blocks * blockSize) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "region file has the wrong size", EXIT_INVALID);
        }
        var nonzero = 0;
        for (i < image.length) if (image[i] != 0) nonzero++;
        Trace.OUT.put2("raw image: %d bytes, %d nonzero", image.length, nonzero).ln();
        def magic = le64(image, RegionHeader.magic.offset);
        if (magic != RegionFormat.MAGIC && RegionFormat.isTagged(magic)) {
                // A setup run by a build of another format version: RegionAllocator
                // refuses to mount it, so it says nothing about the reboot.
                return verdict("INVALID", "the region has another format version: run setup again with this build", EXIT_INVALID);
        }
        if (magic != RegionFormat.MAGIC) {
                // Setup formatted the header with the production provider and
                // closed cleanly, so its loss is not the mutant's doing.
                return verdict("INVALID", "the formatted header is gone: the reserved memory did not survive the reboot", EXIT_INVALID);
        }
        if (le64(image, RegionHeader.userRoot.offset) == 0) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "the sieve root published during setup is gone", EXIT_INVALID);
        }

        def region = openRegion(blocks, blockSize, false);
        if (region == null || region.backendRegion == null) return verdict("LOST", "the region did not mount", EXIT_LOST);
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        Trace.OUT.put1("recovery: %s", region.lastRecovery.name).ln();
        if (region.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.CORRUPT) return verdict("LOST", "the WAL header was corrupt", EXIT_LOST);
        if (region.requiresRecovery()) return verdict("LOST", "the mount latched recovery-required", EXIT_LOST);
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(region, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return verdict("LOST", "the sieve root could not be reattached", EXIT_LOST);
        def cursor = sieve.cursor(), count = sieve.count();
        Trace.OUT.put3("durable cursor %d, primes %d, leaked extents reclaimed %d",
                cursor, count, sieve.leaksReclaimed()).ln();
        Trace.OUT.put3("setup cursor %d, acknowledged cursor %d, acknowledged primes %d",
                setupCursor, ackedCursor, ackedCount).ln();

        def inv = sieve.checkInvariants();
        if (inv != null) return verdict("LOST", Strings.format1("invariant violated: %s", inv), EXIT_LOST);
        if (cursor < ackedCursor) {
                def what = if(cursor <= setupCursor, "every acknowledged step was lost", "some acknowledged steps were lost");
                return verdict("LOST", what, EXIT_LOST);
        }
        if (cursor == ackedCursor && count != ackedCount) {
                return verdict("LOST", "the acknowledged prime count did not survive", EXIT_LOST);
        }
        if (cursor <= MAX_REFERENCE) {
                def expected = referenceCount(cursor);
                if (expected != count) {
                        return verdict("LOST", "the durable prime count disagrees with an independent sieve", EXIT_LOST);
                }
        }
        region.deallocate();
        return verdict("SURVIVED", "all acknowledged steps are durable and every invariant holds", EXIT_OK);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ fault

// One output line in one write, so a script polling the log never sees half.
def say(s: string) {
        System.puts(StringBuilder.new().puts(s).ln().toString());
}

def flag(b: bool) -> u64 {
        return if(b, 1u64, 0u64);
}

def parseFaultBackend(s: string) -> bool {
        if (!parseBackend(s)) return false;
        if (!RebootConfig.usePmem) return true;
        System.puts("the fault modes test a page cache: backend must be \"file\" or \"direct\"\n");
        return false;
}

// The nonce's bytes as they lie in a redo entry (little-endian), in lowercase
// hex, so the script can look for them in a raw copy of the log block.
def HEX = "0123456789abcdef";
def leHex(v: u64) -> string {
        def b = StringBuilder.new();
        var x = v;
        for (i < 8) {
                def octet = int.!(x & 0xFFu64);
                b.putc(HEX[octet >> 4]).putc(HEX[octet & 15]);
                x = x >> 8;
        }
        return b.toString();
}

// Region-relative offset of the marker word (see the header comment).
def markerOffset(region: RegionAllocator, sieve: PWSieve) -> u64 {
        return region.getUserRoot() + u64.!(ChunkHeader.size + SieveRoot.size)
                + (sieve.segmentCapacity() - 1u64) * u64.!(SieveSegment.size)
                + u64.!(SieveSegment.base.offset);
}

// Region-relative offset of the second word: the primeCount field of the
// marker's descriptor, so as unused as the marker itself.
def secondWordOffset(region: RegionAllocator, sieve: PWSieve) -> u64 {
        return markerOffset(region, sieve)
                + u64.!(SieveSegment.primeCount.offset - SieveSegment.base.offset);
}

def hasSpareSlot(sieve: PWSieve) -> bool {
        return sieve.numSegments() + 1u64 < sieve.segmentCapacity();
}

// single: one transaction after a clean close (both slots scrubbed).
// stale-slot and tear: two acknowledged records, N-2 and N-1, left in the
// slots, then N; see the header comment and scripts/desktop-fsyncfail-check.sh.
enum FaultScenario(arg: string, secondWord: string) {
        SINGLE("single", "-"),
        STALE_SLOT("stale-slot", "M2"),
        TEAR("tear", "Z")
}

def parseScenario(s: string) -> (bool, FaultScenario) {
        if (Strings.equal(s, "single")) return (true, FaultScenario.SINGLE);
        if (Strings.equal(s, "stale-slot")) return (true, FaultScenario.STALE_SLOT);
        if (Strings.equal(s, "tear")) return (true, FaultScenario.TEAR);
        System.puts("scenario must be \"single\", \"stale-slot\" or \"tear\"\n");
        return (false, FaultScenario.SINGLE);
}

// The two slots as a newly constructed WAL instance validates them
// (TwoSlotWal.validateSlot(), checksum included), and where they lie. Region-
// relative offsets, which are file offsets.
class SlotView(headerOk: bool, seq0: u64, seq1: u64, slotBytes: u64, slot0: u64, slot1: u64) {
        def seq(slot: int) -> u64 { return if(slot == 0, seq0, seq1); }
        def offset(slot: int) -> u64 { return if(slot == 0, slot0, slot1); }
        def maxSeq() -> u64 { return if(seq0 > seq1, seq0, seq1); }
        def minSeq() -> u64 { return if(seq0 < seq1, seq0, seq1); }
}

// Read the slots through a second backend region over the same file, without
// mounting: no recovery runs and nothing is stored. The WAL constructor with
// initializeFresh false only validates the header and scans the slots. The
// file backend reads what a reopen would read, the page cache; the direct
// backend reads the device through O_DIRECT. Null if the region cannot be
// opened; headerOk false if the region or WAL header is not valid.
def inspectSlots(blocks: u64, blockSize: u64) -> SlotView {
        def size = blocks * blockSize;
        def br = openBackend().open(size, BackendProt.READ.mask | BackendProt.WRITE.mask, OpenMode.ATTACH);
        if (br == null) return null;
        if (checkStorageRegion(br) != null || u64.!(br.range.length) < size) {
                br.close();
                return null;
        }
        def start = u64.view(Pointer.atElement(br.range, 0));
        def magic = RegionUtils.pointerFromU64(start + u64.!(RegionHeader.magic.offset)).load<u64>();
        def logOff = RegionUtils.pointerFromU64(start + u64.!(RegionHeader.logBlock.offset)).load<u64>();
        var view: SlotView;
        if (magic != RegionFormat.MAGIC || logOff == 0u64 || logOff % blockSize != 0u64 || logOff > size - blockSize) {
                view = SlotView.new(false, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0);
        } else {
                def wal = TwoSlotWal.new(start, start + logOff, blockSize, br, OpenMode.ATTACH);
                view = SlotView.new(wal.openOk, wal.slotSeqs[0], wal.slotSeqs[1], wal.slotBytes,
                        wal.slotAddr(0) - start, wal.slotAddr(1) - start);
        }
        br.close();
        return view;
}

def sayView(prefix: string, v: SlotView) {
        if (v == null) {
                say(kvs(StringBuilder.new().puts(prefix).puts("_view").toString(), "unreadable"));
                return;
        }
        say(kv(StringBuilder.new().puts(prefix).puts("_header_valid").toString(), flag(v.headerOk)));
        say(kv(StringBuilder.new().puts(prefix).puts("_slot0_seq").toString(), v.seq0));
        say(kv(StringBuilder.new().puts(prefix).puts("_slot1_seq").toString(), v.seq1));
}

// A random nonce that is neither 0 nor any value in {avoid}; 0 on failure.
def drawDistinct(avoid: Array<u64>) -> u64 {
        for (attempt < 8) {
                def v = randomNonce();
                if (v == 0u64) continue;
                var clash = false;
                for (a in avoid) if (a == v) clash = true;
                if (!clash) return v;
        }
        return 0;
}

def exists(path: string) -> bool {
        def p = Array<byte>.new(path.length + 1);
        for (i < path.length) p[i] = path[i];
        return Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_access, (Pointer.atContents(p), 0)).0 == 0;
}

// Wait for the script to create {path}, checking every 20 ms and for at most
// {seconds}: a script that dies cannot leave this process waiting for ever.
def waitForFile(path: string, seconds: u64) -> bool {
        def ts = Array<long>.new(2);
        def polls = seconds * 50u64;
        for (i = 0u64; i < polls; i++) {
                if (exists(path)) return true;
                ts[0] = 0;
                ts[1] = 20000000;
                Linux.syscall(LinuxConst.SYS_nanosleep, (Pointer.atContents(ts), Pointer.NULL));
        }
        return exists(path);
}

// Read {size} bytes at {offset} through an ordinary descriptor, write the same
// bytes back and fdatasync. Afterwards each of those pages is allocated and
// written on the device -- not a hole, a delayed allocation or an unwritten
// extent -- so the failing write under test needs no allocation, extent
// conversion or other metadata. The bytes do not change.
def rewriteDurably(path: string, offset: u64, size: u64) -> bool {
        def fd = BackingFile.open(path);
        if (fd < 0) return false;
        def buf = Array<byte>.new(int.!(size));
        def ok = DirectIoFileIO.preadFully(fd, Pointer.atContents(buf), size, offset)
                && DirectIoFileIO.pwriteFully(fd, Pointer.atContents(buf), size, offset)
                && BackingFile.fdatasync(fd);
        BackingFile.close(fd);
        return ok;
}

def faultPrepare(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 4) return usage();
        if (!parseFaultBackend(args[3])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def blocks = if(args.length > 4, parseU64(args[4], 512), 512u64);
        def blockSize = if(args.length > 5, parseU64(args[5], 4096), 4096u64);
        def steps = if(args.length > 6, parseU64(args[6], 2), 2u64);
        var scenario = FaultScenario.SINGLE;
        if (args.length > 7) {
                def p = parseScenario(args[7]);
                if (!p.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
                scenario = p.1;
        }
        RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false;
        RebootConfig.path = reservePath(args[1], "wizard-pwfsf");
        if (RebootConfig.path == null) return fail("could not reserve a region file in the directory");
        def state = openState(args[2]);
        if (state == null) return fail("could not create the state file");

        def region = openRegion(blocks, blockSize, true);
        if (region == null || region.txn == null) return fail("could not create the region");
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(region, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return fail("could not lay out the sieve root");
        // Each step commits several transactions, so both log slots hold a
        // record before the clean close below scrubs them.
        for (i < steps) {
                if (!sieve.step()) return fail("setup step failed");
        }
        if (!hasSpareSlot(sieve)) return fail("no spare descriptor slot for the marker word");
        def cursor = sieve.cursor(), count = sieve.count();
        def logOffset = region.getHeader().logBlock;
        def logBytes = region.blockSize;
        def marker = markerOffset(region, sieve);
        def second = secondWordOffset(region, sieve);
        if (region.txn.readU64(region.regionStart + marker) != 0u64) return fail("the marker word is not zero");
        if (region.txn.readU64(region.regionStart + second) != 0u64) return fail("the second word is not zero");
        // Clean close: after-images durable, both slots scrubbed, so the
        // commit under test is sequence 1 of a clean log.
        region.deallocate();

        var pair: Array<string>;
        if (scenario != FaultScenario.SINGLE) {
                pair = preparePair(blocks, blockSize, marker, second);
                if (pair == null) return EXIT_LOST;     // preparePair() printed why
        }
        // The direct backend's format already wrote every unit through
        // O_DIRECT; a buffered rewrite would only leave its pages cached.
        // After preparePair() the reopened mapping is still live: the rewrite
        // goes through the same page cache and changes no byte.
        if (!RebootConfig.useDirect && !rewriteDurably(RebootConfig.path, logOffset, logBytes)) {
                return fail("could not rewrite the log block durably");
        }

        def lines = [
                kvs("region", RebootConfig.path),
                kvs("backend", backendLabel()),
                kvs("scenario", scenario.arg),
                kv("blocks", blocks),
                kv("block_size", blockSize),
                kv("setup_steps", steps),
                kv("setup_cursor", cursor),
                kv("setup_count", count),
                kv("log_offset", logOffset),
                kv("log_bytes", logBytes),
                kv("marker_offset", marker)
        ];
        for (l in lines) {
                if (!state.line(l)) return fail("could not sync the state file");
                say(l);
        }
        if (pair != null) {
                for (l in pair) {
                        if (!state.line(l)) return fail("could not sync the state file");
                        say(l);
                }
        }
        state.close();
        say("OK");
        return EXIT_OK;
}

// stale-slot and tear: after the clean close, mount again and leave two
// acknowledged records, N-2 (M = A) and N-1 (M = B), with their data durable,
// then return without closing. Returns the state lines, or null after printing
// why. The reopened instance is never closed: close() would scrub both slots.
def preparePair(blocks: u64, blockSize: u64, marker: u64, second: u64) -> Array<string> {
        def region = openRegion(blocks, blockSize, false);
        if (region == null || region.txn == null) { fail("the closed region did not mount again"); return null; }
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) { fail(err); return null; }
        if (region.lastRecovery != WalRecovery.CLEAN || region.requiresRecovery()) {
                fail("the cleanly closed region did not mount CLEAN");
                return null;
        }
        def start = region.regionStart;
        if (region.txn.readU64(start + marker) != 0u64 || region.txn.readU64(start + second) != 0u64) {
                fail("the marker or second word is not zero after the clean close");
                return null;
        }
        def a = drawDistinct(Array<u64>.new(0));
        def b = drawDistinct([a]);
        if (a == 0u64 || b == 0u64) { fail("could not draw the values A and B"); return null; }
        // N-2 and N-1, one redo entry each. TransactionCache.commit() applies
        // each after-image once its record is durable.
        region.txn.writeU64(start + marker, a);
        if (!region.txn.commit()) { fail("N-2 was not acknowledged"); return null; }
        region.txn.writeU64(start + marker, b);
        if (!region.txn.commit()) { fail("N-1 was not acknowledged"); return null; }
        // flush() is TwoSlotWal.persistAfterImages(): N-1's applied after-image
        // becomes durable and dataDurableSeq advances; no slot is scrubbed.
        if (!region.txn.flush() || region.requiresRecovery()) { fail("flush() failed"); return null; }
        if (region.txn.readU64(start + marker) != b) { fail("M does not hold B after N-1"); return null; }

        def v = inspectSlots(blocks, blockSize);
        if (v == null || !v.headerOk) { fail("the slots could not be read back"); return null; }
        def seqA = v.minSeq(), seqB = v.maxSeq();
        // A clean log restarts at sequence 1, so N-2 = 1 and N-1 = 2, each in
        // the slot of its parity (validateSlot() checks the parity).
        if (seqA != 1u64 || seqB != 2u64) {
                fail(StringBuilder.new().put2("the slots hold sequences %d and %d, not 1 and 2", v.seq0, v.seq1).toString());
                return null;
        }
        return [
                kv("seq_a", seqA),
                kv("seq_b", seqB),
                kv("value_a", a),
                kvs("value_a_le_hex", leHex(a)),
                kv("value_b", b),
                kvs("value_b_le_hex", leHex(b)),
                kv("word_m_offset", marker),
                kv("word_x_offset", second),
                kv("slot_bytes", v.slotBytes),
                kv("slot0_offset", v.slot0),
                kv("slot1_offset", v.slot1),
                kv("slot0_seq", v.seq0),
                kv("slot1_seq", v.seq1)
        ];
}

def faultCommit(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 6) return usage();
        RebootConfig.path = args[1];
        if (!parseFaultBackend(args[2])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false;
        def bl = requireU64(args[3], "blocks"), bs = requireU64(args[4], "blockSize");
        if (!bl.0 || !bs.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def timeout = if(args.length > 6, parseU64(args[6], 120), 120u64);
        var scenario = FaultScenario.SINGLE;
        if (args.length > 7) {
                def p = parseScenario(args[7]);
                if (!p.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
                scenario = p.1;
        }
        def goCommit = StringBuilder.new().puts(args[5]).puts("/go-commit").toString();
        def goReopen = StringBuilder.new().puts(args[5]).puts("/go-reopen").toString();
        def goNext = StringBuilder.new().puts(args[5]).puts("/go-next").toString();
        if (exists(goCommit) || exists(goReopen) || exists(goNext)) return fail("a go file already exists: use a fresh signal directory");
        if (!exists(RebootConfig.path)) return fail("region file is missing; run fault-prepare first");
        if (scenario != FaultScenario.SINGLE) {
                return faultCommitPair(bl.1, bs.1, scenario, timeout, goCommit, goReopen, goNext);
        }

        def first = openRegion(bl.1, bs.1, false);
        if (first == null || first.txn == null) return fail("the region did not mount");
        def err = checkStorageRegion(first.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        say(kvs("mount_recovery", first.lastRecovery.name));
        if (first.requiresRecovery()) return fail("the first mount latched recovery-required");
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(first, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return fail("could not reattach to the sieve root");
        if (!hasSpareSlot(sieve)) return fail("no spare descriptor slot for the marker word");
        def marker = markerOffset(first, sieve);
        def before = first.txn.readU64(first.regionStart + marker);
        def nonce = randomNonce();
        if (nonce == 0u64 || nonce == before) return fail("could not draw a fresh nonce");
        say(kv("leaks_reclaimed", u64.!(sieve.leaksReclaimed())));
        say(kv("marker_offset", marker));
        say(kv("marker_before", before));
        say(kv("nonce", nonce));
        say(kvs("nonce_le_hex", leHex(nonce)));
        say("READY");
        if (!waitForFile(goCommit, timeout)) return fail("timed out waiting for go-commit; nothing was attempted");

        // The transaction under test: one redo entry, the nonce.
        first.txn.writeU64(first.regionStart + marker, nonce);
        def ok = first.txn.commit();
        say(kv("commit_ok", flag(ok)));
        say(kv("latched", flag(first.requiresRecovery())));
        say("ATTEMPTED");
        if (!waitForFile(goReopen, timeout)) return fail("timed out waiting for go-reopen; the first mount was left unrecovered");

        // Abandon the first mount as the contract requires: no retry, flush or
        // close. Its mapping and descriptor stay open until exit. A new
        // instance opens the region afresh and recovers.
        def second = openRegion(bl.1, bs.1, false);
        if (second == null || second.txn == null) return fail("the reopened region did not mount");
        def err2 = checkStorageRegion(second.backendRegion);
        if (err2 != null) return fail(err2);
        def seen = second.txn.readU64(second.regionStart + marker);
        say(kvs("reopen_recovery", second.lastRecovery.name));
        say(kv("reopen_latched", flag(second.requiresRecovery())));
        say(kv("reopen_marker", seen));
        say(kv("reopen_visible", flag(seen == nonce)));
        say("DONE");
        // Exit without closing either instance, as a crash would.
        return EXIT_OK;
}

// fault-commit for stale-slot and tear. fault-prepare left N-2 and N-1 in the
// slots; the mount replays both, so N = (N-1) + 1 goes to slot N mod 2, over
// N-2's record. The script arms the error target over the 4 KiB page that
// holds that slot and nothing else, from test_slot and the slot offsets
// printed here.
def faultCommitPair(blocks: u64, blockSize: u64, scenario: FaultScenario, timeout: u64,
                goCommit: string, goReopen: string, goNext: string) -> int {
        say(kvs("scenario", scenario.arg));
        def pre = inspectSlots(blocks, blockSize);
        if (pre == null || !pre.headerOk) return fail("the slots could not be read before the mount");
        sayView("pre", pre);
        if (pre.minSeq() == 0u64 || pre.maxSeq() != pre.minSeq() + 1u64) {
                return fail("fault-prepare did not leave two valid records with consecutive sequences");
        }
        def first = openRegion(blocks, blockSize, false);
        if (first == null || first.txn == null) return fail("the region did not mount");
        def err = checkStorageRegion(first.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        say(kvs("mount_recovery", first.lastRecovery.name));
        if (first.lastRecovery != WalRecovery.REPLAYED) return fail("the mount did not replay the two prepared records");
        if (first.requiresRecovery()) return fail("the first mount latched recovery-required");
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(first, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return fail("could not reattach to the sieve root");
        if (!hasSpareSlot(sieve)) return fail("no spare descriptor slot for the marker word");
        // sieve.open() commits only if it reclaims a leak or finishes a
        // retirement; either would write a record and move N.
        if (sieve.leaksReclaimed() != 0) return fail("the mount reclaimed a leaked extent, which commits");
        def marker = markerOffset(first, sieve);
        def second = secondWordOffset(first, sieve);
        def start = first.regionStart;
        def mBefore = first.txn.readU64(start + marker);
        def xBefore = first.txn.readU64(start + second);
        if (mBefore == 0u64) return fail("the marker word holds no prepared value");
        if (xBefore != 0u64) return fail("the second word is not zero");
        def c = drawDistinct([mBefore]);
        def x = drawDistinct([mBefore, c]);
        if (c == 0u64 || x == 0u64) return fail("could not draw fresh values");
        def n = pre.maxSeq() + 1u64;
        def slot = int.!(n % 2u64);
        def lines = [
                kv("leaks_reclaimed", u64.!(sieve.leaksReclaimed())),
                kv("word_m_offset", marker),
                kv("word_x_offset", second),
                kvs("x_word", scenario.secondWord),
                kv("m_before", mBefore),
                kv("x_before", xBefore),
                kv("txn_seq", n),
                kv("test_slot", u64.!(slot)),
                kv("slot_bytes", pre.slotBytes),
                kv("slot0_offset", pre.slot0),
                kv("slot1_offset", pre.slot1),
                kv("value_c", c),
                kvs("value_c_le_hex", leHex(c)),
                kv("value_x", x),
                kvs("value_x_le_hex", leHex(x))
        ];
        for (l in lines) say(l);
        say("READY");
        if (!waitForFile(goCommit, timeout)) return fail("timed out waiting for go-commit; nothing was attempted");

        // Transaction N: M = C; for tear also Z = E, a word N-2 and N-1 never
        // wrote.
        first.txn.writeU64(start + marker, c);
        if (scenario == FaultScenario.TEAR) first.txn.writeU64(start + second, x);
        def ok = first.txn.commit();
        say(kv("commit_ok", flag(ok)));
        say(kv("latched", flag(first.requiresRecovery())));
        say("ATTEMPTED");
        if (!waitForFile(goReopen, timeout)) return fail("timed out waiting for go-reopen; the first mount was left unrecovered");

        // Abandon the first mount, as faultCommit() does. First read the
        // slots as the reopen will.
        def seen = inspectSlots(blocks, blockSize);
        sayView("reopen_seen", seen);
        def again = openRegion(blocks, blockSize, false);
        if (again == null || again.txn == null) return fail("the reopened region did not mount");
        def err2 = checkStorageRegion(again.backendRegion);
        if (err2 != null) return fail(err2);
        say(kvs("reopen_recovery", again.lastRecovery.name));
        say(kv("reopen_latched", flag(again.requiresRecovery())));
        say(kv("reopen_m", again.txn.readU64(again.regionStart + marker)));
        say(kv("reopen_x", again.txn.readU64(again.regionStart + second)));
        if (scenario == FaultScenario.TEAR) {
                say("DONE");
                // Exit without closing either instance, as a crash would.
                return EXIT_OK;
        }

        say("REOPENED");
        if (!waitForFile(goNext, timeout)) return fail("timed out waiting for go-next; next was not attempted");
        // next: one more transaction on the recovered mount, M2 = D, which N
        // did not touch. Only on a mount that recovery left usable.
        def usable = !again.requiresRecovery() && (again.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.REPLAYED
                || again.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.CLEAN);
        if (!usable || seen == null || !seen.headerOk) {
                say(kv("next_ok", 0u64));
                say(kvs("next_skipped", "the reopened mount is not usable"));
                say(kv("next_seq", 0u64));
                say("DONE");
                return EXIT_OK;
        }
        // recover() sets the next sequence to the larger valid one + 1.
        def expected = seen.maxSeq() + 1u64;
        say(kv("next_seq_expected", expected));
        again.txn.writeU64(again.regionStart + second, x);
        def nok = again.txn.commit();
        say(kv("next_ok", flag(nok)));
        say(kv("next_latched", flag(again.requiresRecovery())));
        def after = inspectSlots(blocks, blockSize);
        sayView("next_seen", after);
        // The sequence next got: the newest record now visible, which must be
        // in the slot of its parity (validateSlot() checks that).
        var nextSeq = 0u64;
        if (nok && after != null && after.headerOk) nextSeq = after.maxSeq();
        say(kv("next_seq", nextSeq));
        say("DONE");
        // Exit without closing either instance, as a crash would.
        return EXIT_OK;
}

def faultVerify(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 8) return usage();
        RebootConfig.path = args[1];
        if (!parseFaultBackend(args[2])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false;
        def bl = requireU64(args[3], "blocks"), bs = requireU64(args[4], "blockSize");
        def n = requireU64(args[5], "nonce");
        def sc = requireU64(args[6], "setup-cursor"), sn = requireU64(args[7], "setup-count");
        if (!bl.0 || !bs.0 || !n.0 || !sc.0 || !sn.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def nonce = n.1;

        // As verify: RegionAllocator formats a region whose magic is missing, so look
        // at the raw header before mounting.
        def image = System.fileLoad(RebootConfig.path);
        if (image == null || u64.!(image.length) != bl.1 * bs.1) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "the region file is missing or has the wrong size", EXIT_INVALID);
        }
        if (le64(image, RegionHeader.magic.offset) != RegionFormat.MAGIC) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "the region header's magic is gone", EXIT_INVALID);
        }

        def region = openRegion(bl.1, bs.1, false);
        if (region == null || region.txn == null) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the region did not mount", EXIT_LOST);
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        say(kvs("recovery", region.lastRecovery.name));
        say(kv("latched", flag(region.requiresRecovery())));
        if (region.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.CORRUPT || region.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.PERSIST_FAILED
                || region.requiresRecovery()) {
                return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "recovery left the region unavailable", EXIT_LOST);
        }
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(region, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the sieve root could not be reattached", EXIT_LOST);
        def seen = region.txn.readU64(region.regionStart + markerOffset(region, sieve));
        say(kv("marker", seen));
        say(kv("visible", flag(seen == nonce)));
        say(kv("leaks_reclaimed", u64.!(sieve.leaksReclaimed())));
        def inv = sieve.checkInvariants();
        def alloc = AllocatorInvariants.check(region);
        say(kvs("sieve_invariants", if(inv == null, "ok", inv)));
        say(kvs("allocator_invariants", if(alloc == null, "ok", alloc)));
        def cursor = sieve.cursor(), count = sieve.count();
        say(kv("cursor", cursor));
        say(kv("count", count));
        if (seen != 0u64 && seen != nonce) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the marker holds neither its old value nor the nonce", EXIT_LOST);
        if (inv != null || alloc != null) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "an invariant is violated", EXIT_LOST);
        if (cursor != sc.1 || count != sn.1) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the sieve is not at its setup state", EXIT_LOST);
        if (referenceCount(cursor) != count) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the prime count disagrees with an independent sieve", EXIT_LOST);
        return verdict("CONSISTENT", "recovery succeeded and every invariant holds", EXIT_OK);
}

// A verdict on the two words, with its reason and exit status.
class AckVerdict(kind: string, reason: string, code: int) { }

// The acknowledgement contract for the two-record scenarios
// (docs/persistent-backends.md, "Persistence-failure contract"): every
// acknowledged transaction must be present, and the unacknowledged N either
// wholly present or wholly absent. {m} is the marker word M and {x} the second
// word as found; {a}..{c} and {xv} are the values the transactions stored.
//   stale-slot: N-2 stores M = a, N-1 M = b (acknowledged), N M = c (not),
//     next M2 = xv (acknowledged iff {nextAcked}). M must be b or c; M2 must
//     be xv if next was acknowledged, else 0 or xv.
//   tear: N-2 stores M = a, N-1 M = b (acknowledged), N M = c and Z = xv (not;
//     Z is 0 before N). (M, Z) must be (b, 0) or (c, xv); (b, xv) and (c, 0)
//     are TORN.
// M = a or 0 is LOST in both: N-1's acknowledged write is undone. A value no
// transaction stored is INCONSISTENT. LOST wins over TORN; both reasons are
// given.
def judgeAcks(scenario: FaultScenario, m: u64, x: u64, a: u64, b: u64, c: u64, xv: u64, nextAcked: bool) -> AckVerdict {
        def xName = scenario.secondWord;
        if (m != a && m != b && m != c && m != 0u64) {
                return AckVerdict.new("INCONSISTENT", "M holds a value no transaction stored", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        }
        if (x != 0u64 && x != xv) {
                return AckVerdict.new("INCONSISTENT", Strings.format1("%s holds a value no transaction stored", xName), EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        }
        def mLost = m == a || m == 0u64;
        def mWhy = if(m == a, "M holds N-2's value A: the acknowledged N-1's write is undone",
                "M holds its value from before N-2: the acknowledged N-2 and N-1 are undone");
        if (scenario == FaultScenario.STALE_SLOT) {
                def xLost = nextAcked && x == 0u64;
                if (mLost && xLost) return AckVerdict.new("LOST", Strings.format1("%s, and the acknowledged next's M2 is absent", mWhy), EXIT_LOST);
                if (mLost) return AckVerdict.new("LOST", mWhy, EXIT_LOST);
                if (xLost) return AckVerdict.new("LOST", "the acknowledged next's M2 is absent", EXIT_LOST);
                def nWhy = if(m == c, "N (unacknowledged) is present", "N (unacknowledged) is absent");
                def nextWhy = if(nextAcked, "", if(x == xv, "; next (unacknowledged) is present", "; next (unacknowledged) is absent"));
                return AckVerdict.new("CONSISTENT", StringBuilder.new()
                        .puts("every acknowledged transaction is present; ").puts(nWhy).puts(nextWhy).toString(), EXIT_OK);
        }
        // tear
        def zN = x == xv;
        if (mLost) {
                def tail = if(zN, ", and N's write to Z is present (N is also torn)", "");
                return AckVerdict.new("LOST", StringBuilder.new().puts(mWhy).puts(tail).toString(), EXIT_LOST);
        }
        def mN = m == c;
        if (mN && zN) return AckVerdict.new("CONSISTENT", "N-1 is present; N (unacknowledged) is wholly present", EXIT_OK);
        if (!mN && !zN) return AckVerdict.new("CONSISTENT", "N-1 is present; N (unacknowledged) is wholly absent", EXIT_OK);
        if (zN) return AckVerdict.new("TORN", "N (unacknowledged) is partly present: its Z = E is durable and its M = C is undone (M holds N-1's B)", EXIT_TORN);
        return AckVerdict.new("TORN", "N (unacknowledged) is partly present: its M = C is durable and its Z = E is absent", EXIT_TORN);
}

// A for a, ..., 0 for zero, "other" for anything else: how the summary names a value.
def valueLabel(v: u64, a: u64, b: u64, c: u64, xv: u64, xLabel: string) -> string {
        if (v == 0u64) return "0";
        if (v == a) return "A";
        if (v == b) return "B";
        if (v == c) return "C";
        if (v == xv) return xLabel;
        return "other";
}

def faultVerifyAcks(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 13) return usage();
        RebootConfig.path = args[1];
        if (!parseFaultBackend(args[2])) return EXIT_USAGE;
        RebootConfig.elideWriteback = false;
        def p = parseScenario(args[5]);
        if (!p.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def scenario = p.1;
        if (scenario == FaultScenario.SINGLE) {
                System.puts("fault-verify-acks is for stale-slot and tear; use fault-verify for single\n");
                return EXIT_USAGE;
        }
        def bl = requireU64(args[3], "blocks"), bs = requireU64(args[4], "blockSize");
        def sc = requireU64(args[6], "setup-cursor"), sn = requireU64(args[7], "setup-count");
        def va = requireU64(args[8], "a"), vb = requireU64(args[9], "b"), vc = requireU64(args[10], "c");
        def vx = requireU64(args[11], "x"), ns = requireU64(args[12], "next-seq");
        if (!bl.0 || !bs.0 || !sc.0 || !sn.0 || !va.0 || !vb.0 || !vc.0 || !vx.0 || !ns.0) return EXIT_USAGE;
        def a = va.1, b = vb.1, c = vc.1, xv = vx.1;
        def nextAcked = scenario == FaultScenario.STALE_SLOT && ns.1 != 0u64;
        def xLabel = if(scenario == FaultScenario.STALE_SLOT, "D", "E");
        say(kvs("scenario", scenario.arg));
        say(kv("next_acked", flag(nextAcked)));
        // Every outcome must be distinguishable.
        if (a == 0u64 || b == 0u64 || c == 0u64 || xv == 0u64 || a == b || a == c || a == xv || b == c || b == xv || c == xv) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "the values A, B, C and the second word's are not nonzero and distinct", EXIT_INVALID);
        }

        // As verify: RegionAllocator formats a region whose magic is missing, so look
        // at the raw header before mounting.
        def image = System.fileLoad(RebootConfig.path);
        if (image == null || u64.!(image.length) != bl.1 * bs.1) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "the region file is missing or has the wrong size", EXIT_INVALID);
        }
        if (le64(image, RegionHeader.magic.offset) != RegionFormat.MAGIC) {
                return verdict("INVALID", "the region header's magic is gone", EXIT_INVALID);
        }
        // The records recovery is about to find, through the backend.
        sayView("found", inspectSlots(bl.1, bs.1));

        def region = openRegion(bl.1, bs.1, false);
        if (region == null || region.txn == null) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the region did not mount", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        def err = checkStorageRegion(region.backendRegion);
        if (err != null) return fail(err);
        say(kvs("recovery", region.lastRecovery.name));
        say(kv("latched", flag(region.requiresRecovery())));
        if (region.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.CORRUPT || region.lastRecovery == WalRecovery.PERSIST_FAILED
                || region.requiresRecovery()) {
                return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "recovery left the region unavailable", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        }
        def sieve = PWSieve.new(region, LIVE_SEGMENTS);
        if (!sieve.open()) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the sieve root could not be reattached", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        def m = region.txn.readU64(region.regionStart + markerOffset(region, sieve));
        def x = region.txn.readU64(region.regionStart + secondWordOffset(region, sieve));
        say(kv("m", m));
        say(kv("x", x));
        say(kvs("m_is", valueLabel(m, a, b, c, xv, xLabel)));
        say(kvs("x_is", valueLabel(x, a, b, c, xv, xLabel)));
        say(kv("leaks_reclaimed", u64.!(sieve.leaksReclaimed())));
        def inv = sieve.checkInvariants();
        def alloc = AllocatorInvariants.check(region);
        say(kvs("sieve_invariants", if(inv == null, "ok", inv)));
        say(kvs("allocator_invariants", if(alloc == null, "ok", alloc)));
        def cursor = sieve.cursor(), count = sieve.count();
        say(kv("cursor", cursor));
        say(kv("count", count));
        // The words' verdict is printed whatever the structure's, so a LOST
        // or TORN under a broken invariant is still on record.
        def j = judgeAcks(scenario, m, x, a, b, c, xv, nextAcked);
        say(kvs("acks", j.kind));
        say(kvs("acks_reason", j.reason));
        if (inv != null || alloc != null) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "an invariant is violated", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        if (cursor != sc.1 || count != sn.1) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the sieve is not at its setup state", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        if (referenceCount(cursor) != count) return verdict("INCONSISTENT", "the prime count disagrees with an independent sieve", EXIT_INCONSISTENT);
        return verdict(j.kind, j.reason, j.code);
}

// Every outcome the predictions name, and the others the scripts' tables can
// show, through judgeAcks(), against the verdict each must get.
def faultVerdictTest(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        def a = 0x1111_1111_1111_1111u64, b = 0x2222_2222_2222_2222u64, c = 0x3333_3333_3333_3333u64;
        def d = 0x4444_4444_4444_4444u64, other = 0x5555_5555_5555_5555u64;
        def ss = FaultScenario.STALE_SLOT, tt = FaultScenario.TEAR, z = 0u64;
        // (scenario, M, second word, next acknowledged, expected, what it is)
        def cases: Array<(FaultScenario, u64, u64, bool, string, string)> = [
                (ss, a, d, true, "LOST", "predicted for file: replay of stale N-2 then N+1"),
                (ss, b, d, true, "CONSISTENT", "predicted for direct: N absent, next = N"),
                (ss, c, d, true, "CONSISTENT", "no fault: N and next present"),
                (ss, c, d, false, "CONSISTENT", "next unacknowledged but present"),
                (ss, b, z, false, "CONSISTENT", "next unacknowledged and absent"),
                (ss, c, z, true, "LOST", "acknowledged next absent"),
                (ss, a, z, true, "LOST", "N-1 undone and next absent"),
                (ss, z, d, true, "LOST", "M back to its value before N-2"),
                (ss, other, d, true, "INCONSISTENT", "M holds a foreign value"),
                (ss, b, other, true, "INCONSISTENT", "M2 holds a foreign value"),
                (tt, b, d, false, "TORN", "predicted for file: N's Z durable, its M undone"),
                (tt, b, z, false, "CONSISTENT", "predicted for direct: N wholly absent"),
                (tt, c, d, false, "CONSISTENT", "no fault: N wholly present"),
                (tt, c, z, false, "TORN", "N's M durable, its Z absent"),
                (tt, a, d, false, "LOST", "N-1 undone (and N torn)"),
                (tt, a, z, false, "LOST", "N-1 undone"),
                (tt, z, z, false, "LOST", "M back to its value before N-2"),
                (tt, other, z, false, "INCONSISTENT", "M holds a foreign value"),
                (tt, b, other, false, "INCONSISTENT", "Z holds a foreign value")
        ];
        var failed = 0;
        for (t in cases) {
                def j = judgeAcks(t.0, t.1, t.2, a, b, c, d, t.3);
                def xLabel = if(t.0 == ss, "D", "E");
                def ok = Strings.equal(j.kind, t.4);
                if (!ok) failed++;
                say(StringBuilder.new()
                        .puts(if(ok, "ok   ", "FAIL ")).puts(t.0.arg)
                        .puts(" M=").puts(valueLabel(t.1, a, b, c, d, xLabel))
                        .puts(" ").puts(t.0.secondWord).puts("=").puts(valueLabel(t.2, a, b, c, d, xLabel))
                        .puts(if(t.0 == ss, if(t.3, " next acked", " next unacked"), ""))
                        .puts(" -> ").puts(j.kind).puts(" (expected ").puts(t.4).puts("): ").puts(t.5)
                        .toString());
        }
        say(kv("verdict_cases", u64.!(cases.length)));
        say(kv("verdict_failures", u64.!(failed)));
        return if(failed == 0, EXIT_OK, EXIT_LOST);
}

def main(args: Array<string>) -> int {
        if (args.length < 1 || args[0] == null) return usage();
        def mode = args[0];
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "probe-arm")) return probeArm(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "probe-verify")) return probeVerify(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "setup")) return setup(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "arm")) return arm(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "verify")) return verify(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "fault-prepare")) return faultPrepare(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "fault-commit")) return faultCommit(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "fault-verify")) return faultVerify(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "fault-verify-acks")) return faultVerifyAcks(args);
        if (Strings.equal(mode, "fault-verdict-test")) return faultVerdictTest(args);
        return usage();
}
